  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 117 PM CDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nTonight and Tonight...\n\nWater vapor imagery and objective analysis this afternoon depict mid-\nlevel ridging centered over the Four Corners region and extending \ninto the southern Plains. Along the northern periphery of the ridge, \nwarm temperatures and near-daily thunderstorms chances will continue \nfor the next few days as weak disturbances embedded traverse \nthe region. High temperatures this afternoon are expected to \npeak in the upper 80s to lower 90s.\n\nThunderstorms chances return tonight as a shortwave disturbance \ntracks through central South Dakota towards northeast Nebraska. CAM \nguidance is in fairly good agreement on an MCS developing across \ncentral South Dakota this evening, followed by an east-southeastward \ntrack overnight. The primary uncertainty remains how far south this \nsystem progresses. A more northerly track would largely keep \nconvection across South Dakota into northwest Iowa, while a \nfarther southeastward track could bring a threat for strong to \nlocally severe wind gusts (up to 60 mph) into far northeast \nNebraska early Tuesday morning. \n\nAdditional thunderstorm development is expected across western \nNebraska this evening, with convection pushing eastward during the \nlate evening and early overnight hours. The main question will again \nbe whether storms can remain organized long enough to reach eastern \nNebraska. A strengthening low-level jet nosing into eastern Nebraska \nand far western Iowa overnight may provide enough support to sustain \nconvection. As for both areas of concern, elevated instability will \nbe sufficient, with shear likely the more limiting factor. Bulk \nshear values near 30 kts is generally expected across the area, with \nstronger values focused near and north of the NE/SD border. While an \nisolated pocket of hail (up to 1\") can't be ruled out, damaging wind \ngusts will remain the primary concern for any storms that can remain \nsustained into the area.\n\nTuesday...\n\nAny remnant cloud cover and convective debris should gradually \ndissipate or shift out of the area by late Tuesday morning, allowing \ntemperatures to climb into the low to mid 90s. The warmest \nconditions are expected across southeast Nebraska and southwest \nIowa, closer to the ridging. Continued evapotranspiration and \nlow- level moisture transport will maintain dewpoints in the mid\nto upper 70s, resulting in heat index values of 100-107 \ndegrees. The highest values are expected across southeast NE and\nsouthwest IA, where a Heat Advisory may be needed.\n\nA spotty shower or thunderstorm cannot be ruled out Tuesday \nafternoon, though the better potential arrives Tuesday night into \nearly Wednesday as another shortwave disturbance pivots across SD. \nCAM consistency within the 12Z guidance remains relatively low, as \nis often the case with weakly forced convective regimes. That said, \nanother overnight MCS developing across central SD and tracking east-\nsoutheast towards northeast Nebraska appears plausible.\n\nElevated instability will again be plentiful, with bulk shear \nincreasing to around 35 kts near the NE/SD border. Model soundings \nalso depict relatively dry low-levels and DCAPE values near 1250 \nJ/kg, supporting the potential for strong to severe wind gusts. As \nwith tonight's system, the magnitude of the threat across our area \nwill largely depend on how far south the MCS tracks into eastern NE \nand western IA.\n\nWednesday and Beyond...\n\nAnother hot and humid day is expected Wednesday, with highs in the \nupper 980s to low 90s. A pattern chance begins later Wednesday as a \nmore potent shortwave disturbance ejects from the Front Range into \nthe central Plains, helping to dampen the ridge while bringing \ncooler temperatures and more widespread precipitation chances to the \nregion. Another period of strong to severe storms will again be \npossible on Wednesday evening. \n\nPoPs increase Wednesday evening and peak in the 70-85% range \novernight into Thursday morning. While precipitation chances \ndecrease somewhat during the day Thursday, another shortwave \ndisturbance is expected to bring a renewed round of 50-70% PoPs \nThursday evening into early Friday. Persistent cloud cover and \nperiods of rainfall should keep highs mainly in the mid 70s to mid \n80s from Thursday into the weekend, with intermittent precipitation \nchances continuing through the period. The best rainfall potential \nexists Wednesday night into Thursday, with the NBM bringing a 40-70% \nprobability of at least 1 inch of rainfall during this period, with \nthe highest probabilities focused in a corridor across northeast \nNebraska.",
